#!/bin/sh
#
# script/falsifications: what evidence each Kani harness carries that it can fail (milestone 194).
#
# PROVISIONAL beyond four names. DECISIONS §134 ratified `Falsification:`, the three states
# `replayable`/`attested`/`unfalsified`, this script's name, and the patch path
# `<package>/falsifications/<module.path>.<harness_fn_name>.patch`. Every other spelling here (the
# flags, the column headings) is this lane's and calef names things.
#
# Why this exists. notes/verification.md has said "falsify a property before believing it" since
# milestone 35, and milestone 191 measured what that produced: 141 harnesses and **not one of them
# recording what was done to falsify it**. The rule was rung four, honoured by whoever remembered,
# and the reverse pass found the predictable result. `capability::subset_is_reflexive` proves
# `a & !a == 0`, a tautology no plausible implementation error breaks, and twelve of the 26 `paging`
# harnesses restate six properties once per ISA.
#
# The forcing question is calef's and it is about a claim rather than about code: if this project is
# going to say **proven**, the evidence that the proofs are load-bearing cannot itself be a written
# claim that they are load-bearing. That is one level of indirection away from evidence, and it is
# the same shape as the nine misrecorded roadmap statuses and the fabricated block quote that
# survived twelve days of gates.
#
# **Inductive Validity Cores were checked first and are not available**, which is why this script
# exists in the shape it does. §134 named them as the cheaper family: an IVC is the minimal set of
# model elements a proof actually needed, so it gives coverage with no re-run at all. Neither Kani
# 0.67.0 nor the CBMC 6.8.0 it ships exposes anything of the kind, and the reason is structural
# rather than a missing feature: IVC is a technique of INDUCTIVE model checkers (JKind, Kind2, Sally)
# that prove by k-induction over a transition system, where the core falls out of the unsatisfiable
# induction step. Kani is a BOUNDED model checker that unrolls to a depth and hands one SAT formula
# to CaDiCaL, and a bounded proof has no induction step to take a core from. `cargo kani --coverage`
# is source-based REACHABILITY coverage (which regions symbolic execution touched), which catches
# some vacuity and no tautology at all; `cbmc --slice-formula` drops assignments unrelated to the
# property as a solver optimisation and reports nothing. See notes/verification.md.
#
# The three states, and the unknown one is first-class. This is `script/names`' lesson taken whole,
# and for the same reason: inventing evidence to fill a row puts a false claim in the one record
# whose entire job is saying what is actually known.
#
#   replayable <path>   a patch exists that a script applies to turn this harness red.
#                       --sweep applies it, runs that one harness, REQUIRES red, reverts.
#   attested <date>     a person broke the code and watched it fail; nothing can re-check it.
#                       Counted, and a worklist entry. An assertion is not evidence.
#   unfalsified         nobody has. This is the claim's honest denominator.
#
# And a fourth thing that is not a state, because it is not a harness. A `falsifications/` directory
# may hold a patch against something `cargo kani` cannot run at all: milestone 202 (every confinement
# test is a ritual until somebody breaks the confinement) recorded one against a kernel `#[test_case]`
# that boots under QEMU. It is a real falsification of a real claim and no sweep can replay it until
# milestone 210 (no kernel test can be run by name) lands. Those are printed after the ratio and
# counted in neither half of it, which milestone 212 chose deliberately: a denominator that quietly
# excludes them is the defect, and a numerator that quietly includes evidence nothing re-runs is the
# same defect facing the other way.
#
# The block, which is all the lint checks:
#
#   /// Falsification: replayable `crates/capability/falsifications/verification.subset_is_transitive.patch`
#   /// Falsification: attested 2026-08-30. Flipped the mask; the subset assertion failed.
#   /// Falsification: unfalsified. Nobody has proposed a defect this would catch.
#
# It is the comment run immediately above `#[kani::proof]`, past any other attributes, in the shape
# milestone 115 established for `Name:`: at the thing, never in a registry, so two lanes touching
# two crates cannot collide and a computed report cannot drift from the tree.
#
#   script/falsifications                   the table, by package, and the ratio
#   script/falsifications --unfalsified     the worklist: harnesses with no evidence, biggest first
#   script/falsifications --check           form only, non-zero on a problem (script/lint runs this)
#   script/falsifications --sweep [package] apply every recorded patch, require red, revert
#   script/falsifications --affected-since <base>   sweep only what a diff can reach (the per-PR half)
#
# **The scope is every workspace package, and it was `crates/` until milestone 212.** That is the
# whole of that milestone and it was not an undercount so much as a mislabelled fraction: the ratio
# below is printed as a statement about the tree's proofs, and it described one directory. Milestone
# 193 (put `kernel/src` within reach of the prover) and milestone 197 (`user/` and `xtask` are out of
# reach of the prover) each put a package outside `crates/` into `script/verify`'s crate table, and
# both then grew harnesses and falsification records this script could not see. The package list now
# comes from `cargo metadata`, which is where `script/lint`'s verify-table check already gets the
# same fact, because a hand-maintained list is the same defect one iteration later: `script/verify`
# has recorded that failure twice (`mdns_proto`, then `jh7110_trng`), and it is the invisible
# direction, since a suite whose scope is too small goes green FASTER.
#
# **The gate is presence, never `replayable`.** Nothing here fails a build because a harness has no
# falsification yet, and it must stay that way, for exactly the reason `script/names --check` gates on
# presence rather than on `ratified`: a lint demanding the queue be drained would block every
# unrelated merge behind work nobody can hurry. The worklist is how the queue gets worked; the gate
# only insists a harness say which of the three it is.
#
# **The sweep's posture is `script/mutation`'s, deliberately copied**: a report against a baseline,
# not a per-commit gate, because a full run costs about one `script/verify` and a regression is a
# worklist entry rather than a defect in whatever landed that day. `--affected-since` is the half
# `script/mutation` has no equivalent for, and it is cheap because a falsification runs ONE harness.
#
# BUGS.
#
# - **Nothing forces the ratio upward.** Every harness may sit at `unfalsified` for ever while
#   `--check` stays green. That is the honest cost of making the convention shippable against 141
#   existing harnesses at all, and it is why the number that matters is the ratio rather than the
#   gate.
# - **A recorded falsification proves the harness catches THAT defect, not the class.** It is a
#   floor, and a low one. A patch that flips a comparison proves the assertion is wired to the
#   comparison; it says nothing about the property being the right property.
# - **A patch rots against refactors**, and §134 argues that is correct rather than harmless: a patch
#   that no longer applies means the covered code moved, which is exactly when a falsification should
#   be redone rather than trusted. It is still churn, and a heavily refactored crate re-falsifies
#   often. `--sweep` reports a patch that will not apply as a failure, not as a skip.
# - **`--sweep` refuses to run on a dirty working tree**, because it applies and reverts patches with
#   `git apply` and a failure mid-run would otherwise leave a deliberate defect in somebody's source.
#   That is a real limit: you cannot sweep while you work.
# - **The affected-since computation is crate-granular, not harness-granular.** A change anywhere in
#   a harness crate's dependency closure re-falsifies every replayable harness in that crate, because
#   nothing in this tree knows which lines a given harness actually covers (that is what an IVC would
#   have bought). It fails toward doing too much.
# - **`kernel/src/arch/` carries no harnesses and gains nothing here**, so the architecture layer,
#   where the VisionFive 2's undelivered-wake defect actually lived, is outside this record entirely.
# - **A file's module path is derived from the Cargo target it belongs to, and one shape defeats
#   that.** `user/src` holds 68 `[[bin]]` roots and two single-consumer `#[path]` modules that rule 7
#   permits (`net_transport.rs`, `socket_test_client.rs`). A root contributes no module segment and a
#   `#[path]` module contributes whatever the including file calls it, which need not be its
#   filename. Neither carries a harness today; one that arrived in a `#[path]` module would get a
#   patch path naming the file rather than the module, and `--check` would report it as a path that
#   does not match rather than accept it silently.
# - **Two files carrying `#[kani::proof]` are in no workspace package and are skipped**: the
#   `scripts/kani-lint-shim` source `script/lint` compiles by hand, and `vendor/redoxfs`, kept out of
#   the workspace on purpose. Neither is provable, so neither belongs in a ratio about proofs.
#   `script/lint`'s verify-table check is what keeps that honest, by failing on any harness file
#   belonging to no package with exactly those two argued as exemptions; this script does not keep a
#   second copy of the list.
#
# Name: ratified 2026-08-30 (calef, DECISIONS §134). The `script/` family splits in a way nobody had
# written down until that section: what DOES something is a verb (`verify`, `test`, `fuzz`, `bench`)
# and what REPORTS is a noun (`names`, `citations`, `decisions`, `roadmap`, `coverage`, `mutation`).
# §134 decided a report with a baseline rather than a gate, so this belongs in the noun half. Refused
# `falsify`, whose verb form would promise the gate §134 deliberately did not build.

set -e

ROOT="$(CDPATH='' cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)"

exec python3 - "$ROOT" "$@" <<'PY'
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

root = sys.argv[1]
args = sys.argv[2:]
mode = args[0] if args else ""

STATES = ("replayable", "attested", "unfalsified")

# A `#[kani::proof]` attribute, with or without the `_for_contract` suffix and with or without
# arguments. Matched on the stripped line so indentation inside `mod verification` does not matter.
PROOF = re.compile(r"^#\[kani::proof")
ATTR = re.compile(r"^#\[")
FN = re.compile(r"^(?:pub\s+)?(?:unsafe\s+)?fn\s+(\w+)")
COMMENT = re.compile(r"^//")
RECORD = re.compile(r"^//[/!]?\s*Falsification:\s*(.*)$")

# The first line of a patch that falsifies something `cargo kani` cannot run: `Falsifies
# `kernel::user::c_seam_tests::a_c_out_of_bounds_write_faults...``. See `unclaimed()`.
FALSIFIES = re.compile(r"^Falsifies\s+`?([A-Za-z_][\w:]*)`?", re.M)

MOD_OPEN = re.compile(r"^(?:pub(?:\([^)]*\))?\s+)?mod\s+(\w+)\s*\{")


_META = {}


def metadata(no_deps):
    """`cargo metadata`, cached, because three code paths here want it and it is not cheap.

    This is the whole of milestone 212. The walk used to be `os.walk("crates")`, so the ratio this
    script prints described one directory and was printed as a fraction of the tree. `kernel` and
    `user` are workspace packages at the repository root, both are rows in `script/verify`'s crate
    table since milestones 193 (put `kernel/src` within reach of the prover) and 197 (`user/` and
    `xtask` are out of reach of the prover), and both carry harnesses the old walk could not see.

    Derived rather than listed, for the reason `script/verify`'s own table comment gives twice over:
    `mdns_proto` and then `jh7110_trng` each carried harnesses nothing ran, and that failure is the
    invisible one, because the suite goes green FASTER when a scope is too small.
    """
    key = bool(no_deps)
    if key not in _META:
        cmd = ["cargo", "metadata", "--format-version", "1", "--locked"]
        if no_deps:
            cmd.append("--no-deps")
        _META[key] = json.loads(subprocess.run(
            cmd, cwd=root, capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout)
    return _META[key]


class Package:
    """A workspace package: its name, the directory it lives in, and its Cargo targets.

    The targets are what make the module path right for a package of binaries. `user` is 68
    `[[bin]]` targets sharing one `src/` directory, so `user/src/printenv.rs` is a CRATE ROOT and
    contributes no module segment, where `crates/paging/src/sv39.rs` sits below a `lib` root and
    contributes `sv39`. The old derivation read the path (`parts[2] == "src"`) and would have
    called the first one `printenv::proofs::...`, which is not a path Kani would answer to.
    """

    def __init__(self, name, reldir, targets, has_global_asm):
        self.name = name
        self.reldir = reldir            # "crates/paging", "kernel", "user"
        self.prefix = reldir + "/"
        self.targets = targets          # [(target_name, kind, rel_src_path), ...]
        self.has_global_asm = has_global_asm

    @property
    def bins(self):
        return [t for t in self.targets if t[1] == "bin"]

    def target_of(self, rel):
        """The target whose sources `rel` belongs to, and whether `rel` is that target's root."""
        if rel.endswith(".rs"):
            for t in self.targets:
                if t[2] == rel:
                    return t, True
        best = None
        for t in self.targets:
            d = os.path.dirname(t[2]) + "/"
            if rel.startswith(d) and (best is None or len(d) > len(os.path.dirname(best[2]) + "/")):
                best = t
        return best, False

    def file_mods(self, rel):
        """The module segments a FILE contributes, from the crate root down.

        `crates/paging/src/sv39.rs` under a `lib.rs` root is `["sv39"]`; `user/src/printenv.rs`,
        which IS a root, is `[]`. The rest of a harness's path comes from the inline `mod X {`
        blocks, which is where `verification` and `proofs` come from.
        """
        target, is_root = self.target_of(rel)
        if target is None or is_root:
            return []
        base = os.path.dirname(target[2]) + "/"
        tail = rel[len(base):].split("/")
        tail[-1] = tail[-1][: -len(".rs")]
        if tail[-1] in ("lib", "mod", "main"):
            tail = tail[:-1]
        return tail


def packages():
    """Every workspace package, longest-prefix-first so ownership of a path is unambiguous."""
    meta = metadata(no_deps=True)
    ws = meta["workspace_root"]
    out = []
    for p in meta["packages"]:
        rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.dirname(p["manifest_path"]), ws)
        if rel == "." or rel.startswith(".."):
            continue
        targets = []
        for t in p["targets"]:
            src = os.path.relpath(t["src_path"], ws)
            if src.startswith(".."):
                continue
            targets.append((t["name"], t["kind"][0] if t["kind"] else "?", src))
        # One `cargo kani` flag depends on this and it is derived rather than named, because a
        # package list in this file is the defect milestone 212 exists to remove. Kani refuses a
        # crate containing `global_asm!` outright unless told to ignore it, and `script/verify`
        # carries the same exception for the same reason; see its `kernel)` case and
        # notes/kernel-proofs.md for what the resulting stubs do and do not cover.
        asm = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "grep", "-l", "global_asm!", "--", rel + "/*.rs"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True).returncode == 0
        out.append(Package(p["name"], rel, targets, asm))
    out.sort(key=lambda p: -len(p.prefix))
    return out


def module_path_by_line(file_mods, lines):
    """For each line, the module path from the crate root, as a list of segments.

    Two sources, and both are needed. The **file** contributes what `Package.file_mods` computed
    from the Cargo target it belongs to. The **inline `mod X {` blocks** contribute the rest, which
    is where `verification` comes from, since almost every harness in this tree lives in a
    `#[cfg(kani)] mod verification`.

    Brace counting rather than a Rust parser, which is the right size of tool here and is honest
    about its limit: a brace inside a string or a comment would miscount. Nothing in this tree does
    that at module scope, and a miscount surfaces as a patch path that does not match, which
    `--check` reports rather than silently accepting.
    """
    out = []
    stack = []       # [(depth_at_open, name), ...]
    depth = 0
    for line in lines:
        out.append(file_mods + [n for _, n in stack])
        hit = MOD_OPEN.match(line.strip())
        opened = hit.group(1) if hit else None
        for ch in line:
            if ch == "{":
                depth += 1
                if opened is not None:
                    stack.append((depth, opened))
                    opened = None
            elif ch == "}":
                while stack and stack[-1][0] == depth:
                    stack.pop()
                depth -= 1
    return out


def harness_files():
    """Every tracked `.rs` file carrying a `#[kani::proof]`, from `git grep` rather than a walk.

    Two files in this tree hold the attribute and belong to no workspace package: the
    `scripts/kani-lint-shim` source `script/lint` compiles by hand for its harness-clippy pass, and
    `vendor/redoxfs`, upstream code kept out of the workspace on purpose. Neither is provable, so
    neither belongs in a ratio about proofs. They are skipped here rather than listed, because
    `script/lint` already gates that direction: its verify-table check reports any file carrying
    `#[kani::proof]` that belongs to no workspace package, with those two named as the argued
    exemptions. A second copy of that list here would be a second thing to keep true.
    """
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "grep", "-lE", r"#\[kani::proof", "--", "*.rs"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True)
    return sorted(out.stdout.split())


def harnesses():
    """Every Kani harness in a workspace package, with the falsification record it carries.

    Derived from the tree rather than from a list, for the reason `script/verify`'s own scope
    computation gives: a harness that arrives tomorrow is covered without editing anything, and a
    list that is one entry short fails silently and invisibly.
    """
    pkgs = packages()
    out = []
    for rel in harness_files():
        pkg = next((p for p in pkgs if rel.startswith(p.prefix)), None)
        if pkg is None:
            continue
        path = os.path.join(root, rel)
        lines = open(path, errors="replace").read().split("\n")
        mods = module_path_by_line(pkg.file_mods(rel), lines)
        target, _ = pkg.target_of(rel)
        for i, line in enumerate(lines):
            if not PROOF.match(line.strip()):
                continue
            # Downward past any further attributes (`#[kani::unwind(8)]`, `#[kani::solver(..)]`)
            # to the function this proves.
            name = None
            for j in range(i + 1, len(lines)):
                s = lines[j].strip()
                if not s or ATTR.match(s) or COMMENT.match(s):
                    continue
                hit = FN.match(s)
                name = hit.group(1) if hit else None
                break
            if name is None:
                out.append(Harness(pkg, target, "?", rel, i + 1, None, None, mods[i]))
                continue
            # Upward past the other attributes to the comment run above them, which is where
            # the block lives. Blank lines end it: a comment separated from the harness by a
            # gap belongs to whatever is above the gap.
            record, record_line = None, None
            k = i - 1
            while k >= 0 and ATTR.match(lines[k].strip()):
                k -= 1
            while k >= 0 and COMMENT.match(lines[k].strip()):
                hit = RECORD.match(lines[k].strip())
                if hit:
                    record, record_line = hit.group(1).strip(), k + 1
                k -= 1
            out.append(Harness(pkg, target, name, rel, i + 1, record, record_line, mods[i]))
    out.sort(key=lambda h: (h.package, h.name))
    return out


class Harness:
    def __init__(self, pkg, target, name, rel, line, record, record_line, mods):
        self.pkg = pkg
        self.package = pkg.name
        self.target = target
        self.mods = mods
        self.name = name
        self.rel = rel
        self.line = line
        self.record = record
        self.record_line = record_line

    @property
    def state(self):
        """The first word of the record, with any trailing punctuation off it.

        The strip is load-bearing rather than tidy, and milestone 202 found it by writing the
        block this script's own header offers as an example: `unfalsified. Nobody has proposed a
        defect this would catch.` `attested` already tolerated the prose after its date, so only
        the bare states rejected the documented form, and the rejection read as a state nobody
        defined rather than as a punctuation rule. A convention whose reference example fails its
        own gate teaches the wrong thing to whoever hits it first.
        """
        if self.record is None:
            return None
        words = self.record.split()
        return words[0].rstrip(".,;:") if words else None

    @property
    def patch(self):
        """The repo-relative patch path a `replayable` record names, or None."""
        if self.state != "replayable":
            return None
        parts = self.record.split()
        # Backticks are stripped rather than required or forbidden. The path must be backticked in
        # a `///` doc comment or `clippy::doc_markdown` rejects it as an unmarked item path, and
        # `script/lint`'s kani-shim pass compiles exactly these `#[cfg(kani)]` modules, so it is the
        # gate that finds it. A `//` comment has no such rule. Accepting both keeps one form in the
        # record rather than one form per comment style.
        return parts[1].strip("`") if len(parts) > 1 else None

    @property
    def qualified(self):
        """The harness's fully qualified name inside its crate, `::`-joined.

        This is the string `cargo kani --harness ... --exact` wants, which is why the patch path is
        keyed on it: the path and the sweep's own filter are one fact with the separators changed.
        """
        return "::".join(self.mods + [self.name])

    @property
    def conventional_patch(self):
        # DECISIONS §134 as amended 2026-08-31 and clarified 2026-09-01:
        # `<package>/falsifications/<module.path>.<harness_fn_name>.patch`, with the module path
        # ALWAYS included and no branch. The first spelling keyed on the function name alone, which
        # `paging` broke on contact: six properties stated once per ISA share six function names
        # across `aarch64.rs`, `sv39.rs` and `x86_64.rs`, so three harnesses would collide on one
        # file. Qualifying only when a name repeats was refused for being a branch on an unstable
        # property, since a harness added elsewhere would retroactively invalidate an existing path.
        #
        # The directory is the PACKAGE's, not `crates/<crate>`: `kernel` and `user` are packages at
        # the repository root and both already hold `falsifications/`, which §134's clarification
        # recognises.
        return "%s/falsifications/%s.patch" % (
            self.pkg.reldir, ".".join(self.mods + [self.name]))

    def kani_argv(self):
        """What `cargo kani` needs to run this ONE harness and nothing else.

        Two package-shaped facts, both derived. A package of many binaries needs `--bin`, because
        `cargo kani -p user` compiles all 68 and fails on the first `#![no_std]` root that does not
        mention Kani; `script/verify` solves the same problem by deriving a `--bin` list, and a
        sweep wants exactly one. A package carrying `global_asm!` needs it ignored, or Kani refuses
        to compile the crate at all.
        """
        argv = ["cargo", "kani", "-p", self.package]
        if self.pkg.has_global_asm:
            argv += ["-Z", "unstable-options", "--ignore-global-asm"]
        if self.target is not None and self.target[1] == "bin" and len(self.pkg.bins) > 1:
            argv += ["--bin", self.target[0]]
        return argv + ["--harness", self.qualified, "--exact", "--output-format=terse"]


def patch_files(pkgs):
    """Every patch under a package's `falsifications/`, repo-relative."""
    out = []
    for pkg in sorted(pkgs, key=lambda p: p.reldir):
        d = os.path.join(root, pkg.reldir, "falsifications")
        if not os.path.isdir(d):
            continue
        for f in sorted(os.listdir(d)):
            if f.endswith(".patch"):
                out.append("%s/falsifications/%s" % (pkg.reldir, f))
    return out


def unclaimed(path, pkgs):
    """Classify a patch no harness claims: a known gap, or rot.

    §134 checks both directions, and the reason a patch with no harness is normally a PROBLEM is
    rot: the harness was renamed or deleted and the patch is now a file nobody runs.

    **One patch in this tree is neither**, and pretending otherwise would put a hardcoded exemption
    in the script whose whole milestone was removing a hardcoded scope. Milestone 202 (every
    confinement test is a ritual until somebody breaks the confinement) recorded a falsification of
    `kernel::user::c_seam_tests::a_c_out_of_bounds_write_faults_and_changes_nothing_outside_its_grant`,
    which is a `#[test_case]` that boots under QEMU rather than a Kani harness. It is a real record
    of a real falsification and nothing can sweep it until milestone 210 (no kernel test can be run
    by name) lands.

    So the distinction is drawn mechanically rather than by name. A patch may open with
    `Falsifies `<fully::qualified::name>``, and it is a **known gap** when that name agrees with the
    filename and resolves to a function that exists in the package and carries no `#[kani::proof]`.
    Everything else is rot. That keeps the rot check sharp in the direction that matters: a renamed
    or deleted harness fails, because the name no longer resolves to a function at all.

    Returns (kind, detail), where kind is "gap" or "rot".
    """
    body = open(os.path.join(root, path), errors="replace").read()
    head = body.split("diff --git")[0]
    hit = FALSIFIES.search(head)
    stem = os.path.basename(path)[: -len(".patch")]
    pkgdir = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(path))
    pkg = next((p for p in pkgs if p.reldir == pkgdir), None)
    if hit is None or pkg is None:
        return "rot", "no harness claims it and it declares no `Falsifies` target"
    target = hit.group(1)
    segments = target.split("::")
    if segments and segments[0] == pkg.name:
        segments = segments[1:]
    if ".".join(segments) != stem:
        return "rot", ("it declares `Falsifies %s`, which spells the filename `%s.patch`, not `%s`"
                       % (target, ".".join(segments), os.path.basename(path)))
    fn = segments[-1] if segments else ""
    found = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", root, "grep", "-lE",
         r"fn[[:space:]]+%s[[:space:]]*[(<]" % re.escape(fn), "--", pkg.reldir],
        capture_output=True, text=True)
    if found.returncode != 0:
        return "rot", ("it falsifies `%s`, and no function of that name exists in `%s` any more"
                       % (target, pkg.reldir))
    return "gap", "%s (%s), which no Kani harness declares" % (target, found.stdout.split()[0])


def check(hs, pkgs):
    """Form, never truth. Exactly the line `script/names --check` draws, and for the same reason.

    Both directions, which §134 asked for by name: every `replayable` path resolves AND every patch
    under a package's `falsifications/` has a harness that claims it. A one-directional check lets a
    patch rot into a file nobody runs, which is the failure this record exists to end wearing
    different clothes.
    """
    problems = []
    claimed = {}
    # A duplicate qualified name is impossible in Rust, so this is a guard on the PARSER rather than
    # on the tree: if brace counting ever miscomputes a module path, two harnesses collapse onto one
    # patch path and the sweep would prove the wrong one. Cheap to assert, invisible if it drifts.
    seen = {}
    for h in hs:
        seen[h.conventional_patch] = seen.get(h.conventional_patch, 0) + 1
    for h in hs:
        where = "%s:%d" % (h.rel, h.line)
        if h.name == "?":
            problems.append("%s: a `#[kani::proof]` whose function this script could not find"
                            % where)
            continue
        if h.record is None:
            problems.append("%s: harness `%s` carries no `Falsification:` block. One of: "
                            "`replayable <path>`, `attested <YYYY-MM-DD>`, `unfalsified`."
                            % (where, h.name))
            continue
        state = h.state
        if state not in STATES:
            problems.append("%s: harness `%s` has state `%s`; the three are %s"
                            % (where, h.name, state, ", ".join("`%s`" % s for s in STATES)))
            continue
        if state == "replayable":
            if seen[h.conventional_patch] > 1:
                problems.append(
                    "%s: %d harnesses compute the patch path `%s`, which cannot happen in Rust and "
                    "so means this script's module-path tracking is wrong. Do not record a "
                    "falsification against it until that is fixed; the sweep would prove the wrong "
                    "harness." % (where, seen[h.conventional_patch], h.conventional_patch))
                continue
            path = h.patch
            if not path:
                problems.append("%s: harness `%s` is `replayable` and names no patch" % (where, h.name))
            elif path != h.conventional_patch:
                problems.append("%s: harness `%s` names `%s`; §134 spells it `%s`"
                                % (where, h.name, path, h.conventional_patch))
            elif not os.path.exists(os.path.join(root, path)):
                problems.append("%s: harness `%s` names `%s`, which does not exist"
                                % (where, h.name, path))
            else:
                claimed[path] = h.name
                body = open(os.path.join(root, path), errors="replace").read()
                head = body.split("diff --git")[0].strip()
                if "diff --git" not in body:
                    problems.append("%s: `%s` holds no `diff --git` hunk for `git apply`" % (where, path))
                elif not head:
                    problems.append(
                        "%s: `%s` opens with no prose. A falsification that makes a harness fail "
                        "for the wrong reason proves nothing, so the patch must say which property "
                        "it expects to break, above the first `diff --git` line (git apply ignores it)."
                        % (where, path))
        elif state == "attested":
            rest = h.record.split(None, 1)
            date = rest[1].split(".")[0].strip() if len(rest) > 1 else ""
            if not re.match(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$", date):
                problems.append("%s: harness `%s` is `attested` with no `YYYY-MM-DD` date"
                                % (where, h.name))
    gaps = []
    for p in patch_files(pkgs):
        if p in claimed:
            continue
        kind, detail = unclaimed(p, pkgs)
        if kind == "gap":
            gaps.append((p, detail))
        else:
            fn = os.path.basename(p)[: -len(".patch")]
            problems.append("%s: %s. §134 checks both directions: a patch is named "
                            "`<module.path>.<harness_fn_name>.patch` and the harness `%s` must "
                            "carry `Falsification: replayable %s`." % (p, detail, fn, p))
    return problems, gaps


def table(hs, pkgs):
    counts = {s: 0 for s in STATES}
    by_pkg = {}
    for h in hs:
        counts[h.state] = counts.get(h.state, 0) + 1
        by_pkg.setdefault(h.package, []).append(h)
    width = max(len(h.name) for h in hs) if hs else 10
    for pkg in sorted(by_pkg):
        print("%s" % pkg)
        for h in by_pkg[pkg]:
            rec = h.record or "(no block)"
            print("  %-*s  %s" % (width, h.name, rec))
        print("")
    total = len(hs)
    evidence = counts.get("replayable", 0)
    print("%d harnesses in %d packages" % (total, len(by_pkg)))
    for s in STATES:
        n = counts.get(s, 0)
        print("  %-12s %3d  %4.0f%%" % (s, n, 100.0 * n / total if total else 0))
    print("")
    # The one number §134 asked this script to print, in the sentence it is honest in. Everything
    # else here is a means to it.
    print("%d of %d harnesses (%.0f%%) carry evidence a machine can replay." % (evidence, total, 100.0 * evidence / total if total else 0))
    # Records that exist, that a person performed, and that no sweep can replay, printed rather
    # than folded into either number above. Milestone 212 decided this explicitly: a denominator
    # that quietly excludes them is what it was written to end, and so is a numerator that quietly
    # includes evidence nothing re-runs.
    _, gaps = check(hs, pkgs)
    if gaps:
        print("")
        print("%d falsification record(s) nothing here can sweep, because the thing they falsify is"
              % len(gaps))
        print("not a Kani harness. They are outside both numbers above:")
        for p, detail in gaps:
            print("  %s" % p)
            print("      %s" % detail)

def unfalsified(hs):
    """The worklist. Biggest package first, because §134's own examples of chaff are the crates with
    the most harnesses, and because falsifying two harnesses in one crate is one context load."""
    rows = [h for h in hs if h.state in (None, "unfalsified")]
    by_pkg = {}
    for h in rows:
        by_pkg.setdefault(h.package, []).append(h)
    for pkg in sorted(by_pkg, key=lambda c: (-len(by_pkg[c]), c)):
        print("%s  (%d)" % (pkg, len(by_pkg[pkg])))
        for h in by_pkg[pkg]:
            print("  %s  %s:%d" % (h.name, h.rel, h.line))
    print("")
    print("%d of %d harnesses have no falsification." % (len(rows), len(hs)))


def dirty():
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "status", "--porcelain"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip()
    return out


def sweep(hs, only=None):
    """Apply each recorded patch, run that ONE harness, require red, revert.

    A survivor here is a harness whose own recorded falsification failed to make it fail, which is
    exactly the signal nothing in this tree could previously detect.
    """
    d = dirty()
    if d:
        print("script/falsifications --sweep refuses to run on a dirty working tree: it applies and")
        print("reverts patches with `git apply`, and a failure mid-run would leave a deliberate")
        print("defect in your source. Commit or set the change aside first.")
        print("")
        print(d)
        return 2
    todo = [h for h in hs if h.state == "replayable" and (only is None or h.package in only)]
    if not todo:
        print("nothing to sweep" + (" in %s" % ", ".join(sorted(only)) if only else ""))
        return 0
    survivors, broken = [], []
    for h in todo:
        patch = os.path.join(root, h.patch)
        print("==> %s::%s" % (h.package, h.name))
        applied = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "apply", patch],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True)
        if applied.returncode != 0:
            # A patch that will not apply is a FAILURE and not a skip. §134's claim is that this rot
            # is the mechanism working: the covered code moved, so the falsification is stale and
            # must be redone rather than trusted.
            print("    STALE    the patch no longer applies; the covered code moved")
            print("    " + applied.stderr.strip().replace("\n", "\n    "))
            broken.append(h)
            continue
        try:
            # `--exact` against the fully qualified name, which is what the amended patch path
            # buys beyond uniqueness: the path and this filter are one fact with the separators
            # changed. Before the amendment this was a substring match on the bare function name,
            # and `paging`'s three `index_is_always_in_bounds` harnesses would all have run, so
            # "one of them went red" proved nothing about the one being falsified.
            run = subprocess.run(h.kani_argv(), cwd=root, capture_output=True, text=True)
            out = run.stdout + run.stderr
            hit = re.search(r"(\d+) successfully verified harnesses, (\d+) failures, (\d+) total",
                            out)
            if not hit:
                print("    ERROR    could not read Kani's summary; ran with exit %d" % run.returncode)
                last = out.strip().splitlines()
                if last:
                    print("    " + last[-1])
                broken.append(h)
            elif hit.group(3) != "1":
                # Belt and braces over `--exact`: if Kani ever matched none (a renamed harness) or
                # more than one, "something went red" would prove nothing about this harness.
                print("    ERROR    `--exact %s` matched %s harnesses, not 1"
                      % (h.qualified, hit.group(3)))
                broken.append(h)
            elif hit.group(2) == "1":
                print("    red      the harness caught it")
            else:
                print("    SURVIVOR the harness stayed green with its own falsification applied")
                survivors.append(h)
        finally:
            rev = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "apply", "-R", patch],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True)
        if rev.returncode != 0:
            # Stop here rather than continue. Every later harness would be proved against a tree
            # holding a deliberate defect, and a green result from that means nothing.
            print("    COULD NOT REVERT %s; your tree is modified" % h.patch)
            print("    " + rev.stderr.strip())
            return 3
    print("")
    print("%d swept, %d survivors, %d stale or broken" % (len(todo), len(survivors), len(broken)))
    for h in survivors:
        print("  survivor  %s::%s" % (h.package, h.name))
    for h in broken:
        print("  stale     %s::%s" % (h.package, h.name))
    return 1 if (survivors or broken) else 0


def affected_packages(base, hs):
    """Which harness packages a diff can reach, by the rule `script/verify --affected-since` uses.

    Package-granular on purpose and stated in BUGS: nothing here knows which lines a harness covers,
    so a change inside a harness package's dependency closure re-falsifies every replayable harness
    in it. It fails toward doing too much, which for a run measured in seconds is the right
    direction.
    """
    changed = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "diff", "--name-only", base, "HEAD"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.split()
    meta = metadata(no_deps=False)
    ws = meta["workspace_root"]
    pkgs = {}
    for p in meta["packages"]:
        rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.dirname(p["manifest_path"]), ws)
        if rel == "." or rel.startswith(".."):
            continue
        pkgs[p["id"]] = (p["name"], rel + "/")

    def owner(path):
        best = None
        for pid, (_, prefix) in pkgs.items():
            if path.startswith(prefix) and (best is None or len(prefix) > len(pkgs[best][1])):
                best = pid
        return best

    changed_ids = set()
    unattributable = False
    for f in changed:
        if re.match(r"^(notes/|design/|[A-Z_]+\.md$|script/|scripts/|\.github/)", f):
            continue
        pid = owner(f)
        if pid is None:
            unattributable = True
        else:
            changed_ids.add(pid)
    edges = {n["id"]: [d["pkg"] for d in n.get("deps", [])] for n in meta["resolve"]["nodes"]}
    carriers = {h.package for h in hs}
    out = set()
    for pid, (name, _) in pkgs.items():
        if name not in carriers:
            continue
        if unattributable:
            out.add(name)
            continue
        closure, stack = set(), [pid]
        while stack:
            cur = stack.pop()
            if cur in closure:
                continue
            closure.add(cur)
            stack.extend(edges.get(cur, []))
        if closure & changed_ids:
            out.add(name)
    return out


pkgs = packages()
hs = harnesses()
if not hs:
    sys.exit("script/falsifications: found no Kani harnesses in any workspace package, which cannot "
             "be right")

if mode == "--check":
    problems, gaps = check(hs, pkgs)
    for p in problems:
        print("  " + p)
    if problems:
        print("")
        print("%d problem(s). See the header of script/falsifications and DECISIONS §134." % len(problems))
        sys.exit(1)
    n = sum(1 for h in hs if h.state == "replayable")
    print("  %d harnesses in %d packages, every one carrying a Falsification: block (%d replayable)"
          % (len(hs), len({h.package for h in hs}), n))
    for p, _ in gaps:
        print("  %s falsifies something that is not a Kani harness; nothing sweeps it" % p)
elif mode == "--unfalsified":
    unfalsified(hs)
elif mode == "--sweep":
    sys.exit(sweep(hs, set(args[1:]) or None))
elif mode == "--affected-since":
    if len(args) < 2:
        sys.exit("usage: script/falsifications --affected-since <base-ref>")
    names = affected_packages(args[1], hs)
    if not names:
        print("no harness package is reachable from this change; nothing to re-falsify")
        sys.exit(0)
    print("reachable harness packages: %s" % ", ".join(sorted(names)))
    sys.exit(sweep(hs, names))
elif mode in ("", "--all"):
    table(hs, pkgs)
else:
    sys.exit("script/falsifications: unknown option %r (see the header)" % mode)
